    <GaugingStation location="Kenmore" name="Tay" number="15016">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity area station in the upper Tay, 2km d/s from Loch Tay.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Cableway present. Natural control. All flows contained to date. Strong winds over Loch Tay (2km upstream) can affect flows. Current ratings derived from current meter gaugings. Flow estimates above the highest gauging are based on simple extrapolation. Multiple peak flow ratings across period of record.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity area station, about 60m wide.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Daily gaugeboard readings 1959-1974. Naturalised monthly flows available 1974-1985. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2021 (WINFAP Files v10) and in August 2024 (WINFAP Files v13).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Slow reacting regime. 120 sq km of the catchment is controlled for hydroelectric power. Water imported from Lyon catchment since 1958 - evident in water balance. Water exported to Loch Earn catchment since 1958.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">The catchment is in the Grampians (rising up to &gt;1200m) and is steeply sloping except for the valley bottom. Mountainous, often snowy in winter. Bedrock almost all metamorphic; half overlain by superficial deposits. Land use rough grazing with forest. Catchment contains Loch Tay (16 miles long, 1.5miles wide) and some smaller lochs upstream. Hydro-electric power schemes have developed in the catchment.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">H</Comment>
        </Comments>
        <Usages>
            <Usage notes="Well gauged to QMED." suitable="Yes" type="QMED"/>
            <Usage notes="Good spread of gaugings through the flow range and flows are largely contained in channel." suitable="Yes" type="Pooling"/>
        </Usages>
    </GaugingStation>
